1
00:00:14,270 --> 00:00:17,010
2.3 Organizational Systems.

2
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:20,660
Projects operate within the
constraints imposed by the

3
00:00:20,661 --> 00:00:24,250
organization through their
structure and governance framework.

4
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:30,630
To operate effectively and efficiently,
the project manager needs to understand

5
00:00:30,631 --> 00:00:35,930
where responsibility, accountability and
authority reside within the organization.

6
00:00:36,510 --> 00:00:41,190
This understanding will help the project
manager effectively use his or her power,

7
00:00:41,191 --> 00:00:44,763
influence, competence,
leadership and political

8
00:00:44,764 --> 00:00:47,770
capabilities to successfully
complete the project.

9
00:00:48,190 --> 00:00:53,770
The interaction of multiple factors within
an individual organisation creates a

10
00:00:53,771 --> 00:00:57,810
unique system that impacts the project
operating in that system.

11
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:02,670
The resulting organisational system
determines the power, influence,

12
00:01:03,630 --> 00:01:06,597
interests, competence
and political capabilities of

13
00:01:06,598 --> 00:01:09,270
the people who are able
to act within the system.

14
00:01:09,271 --> 00:01:13,863
The system factors,
management elements, governance

15
00:01:13,864 --> 00:01:17,150
frameworks and
organisational structure types.

16
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:19,450
That's from the PIMBOC Guide, page 42.

17
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:23,570
The first question that we need to answer
is, what is the system?

18
00:01:24,410 --> 00:01:30,550
The Oxford Dictionary defines a system as
a set of things working together as parts

19
00:01:30,551 --> 00:01:35,390
of a mechanism or an interconnecting
network, a complex whole.

20
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:39,250
The PIMBock defines a system as a
collection of various.

21
00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:42,696
various components that
together can produce results

22
00:01:42,697 --> 00:01:45,670
not obtainable by the
individual components alone.

23
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:51,550
The principles related to systems are
systems are dynamic.

24
00:01:52,510 --> 00:01:53,970
Systems can be optimized.

25
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,810
System components can be optimized.

26
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:01,048
Systems and their components
cannot be optimized at the

27
00:02:01,049 --> 00:02:06,450
same time and systems are
non-linear in responsiveness, i.e.

28
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:11,150
a change in the input does not produce a
predictable change in the output.

29
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:17,510
The system's dynamics are defined by the
interaction between the components based

30
00:02:17,511 --> 00:02:21,490
on the relationships and dependencies that
exist between the components.

31
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:26,330
Typically an organization's management is
responsible for its systems.

32
00:02:27,030 --> 00:02:32,830
They examine the optimum trade-offs
between the components and the system in

33
00:02:32,831 --> 00:02:35,823
order to take the appropriate
action in modifying the

34
00:02:35,824 --> 00:02:38,590
systems to achieve the best
outcomes for the organization.

35
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:46,670
These systems impact the organization's
projects and so the project manager must

36
00:02:46,671 --> 00:02:51,470
consider these systems as well as the
organization's governance framework when

37
00:02:51,471 --> 00:02:54,750
determining how to fulfill the project's
objectives.

38
00:02:55,650 --> 00:02:57,790
Organizational governance frameworks.

39
00:02:58,510 --> 00:03:04,610
Recent PMI research reveals that
governance refers to organizational or

40
00:03:04,611 --> 00:03:08,330
structural arrangements at all levels of
an organization.

41
00:03:08,331 --> 00:03:13,190
designed to determine and influence the
behaviour of the organisation's members.

42
00:03:13,990 --> 00:03:19,050
This research suggests that the concept of
governance is multidimensional and

43
00:03:19,051 --> 00:03:25,150
includes consideration of people,
roles, structures and policies and

44
00:03:25,151 --> 00:03:30,370
requires providing direction and oversight
through data and feedback.

45
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:32,850
That's from PINBULK guide, page 43.

46
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:34,970
What is governance?

47
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:41,930
It's a framework within which an
organisation exercises its authority and

48
00:03:41,931 --> 00:03:49,804
includes rules, policies, procedures,
norms, relationships, systems and processes.

49
00:03:50,370 --> 00:03:56,590
This framework influences how objectives
of the organisation are set and achieved,

50
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:01,490
risk is monitored and assessed and
performance is optimised.

51
00:04:02,110 --> 00:04:06,210
Two, governance of portfolios,
programs and projects.

52
00:04:07,210 --> 00:04:12,070
As a member of PMI you can download a copy
of many publications free of charge.

53
00:04:12,410 --> 00:04:16,270
For this section you will find the
following publication of value.

54
00:04:16,930 --> 00:04:22,170
It's the governance or portfolio programs
and projects, a practice guide.

55
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:26,110
This practice guide describes four
governance domains of alignment,

56
00:04:26,710 --> 00:04:28,560
risk, performance and communications.

57
00:04:29,150 --> 00:04:32,690
You can read more about it on the PIMBOC
guide page 44.

58
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:35,430
Number three, management elements.

59
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:40,190
These are the key functions or principles
of general management in the organisation

60
00:04:40,191 --> 00:04:45,450
and are allocated within the organisation
according to its governance structure and

61
00:04:45,451 --> 00:04:47,850
the organisational structure types
selected.

62
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:55,330
They include division of work using
specialised skills and availability to

63
00:04:55,331 --> 00:05:01,130
perform work, authority given to perform
work, responsibility to perform work

64
00:05:01,131 --> 00:05:04,670
appropriately assigned based on such
action.

65
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:06,570
attributes of skill and experience.

66
00:05:07,630 --> 00:05:09,150
Discipline of action, e.g.

67
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:12,150
respect for authority, people and rules.

68
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:16,544
Unity of command, e.g.,
only one person gives

69
00:05:16,545 --> 00:05:20,124
orders for any action or
activity to an individual.

70
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:24,370
The unity of direction, e.g.

71
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:29,950
one plan and one head for a group of
activities with the same objective.

72
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:34,590
General goals of the organization take
precedence over individuals.

73
00:05:34,591 --> 00:05:35,424
goals.

74
00:05:36,050 --> 00:05:41,690
They are to be paid fairly for work
performed, optimal use of resources,

75
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:46,370
clear communications channels,
write materials to the right person for

76
00:05:46,371 --> 00:05:50,790
the right job at the right time,
fair and equal treatment of people in the

77
00:05:50,791 --> 00:05:56,450
workplace, clear security of work
positions, safety of people in the

78
00:05:56,451 --> 00:05:59,815
workplace, open
contribution to planning and

79
00:05:59,816 --> 00:06:03,290
execution by each
person, and optimal morale.

80
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:08,010
The PIMBOC guide says in page 45,
performance of these management elements

81
00:06:08,011 --> 00:06:11,270
are assigned to selected individuals
within the organisation.

82
00:06:12,270 --> 00:06:14,676
These individuals might perform the noted

83
00:06:14,677 --> 00:06:18,064
functions within various
organisational structures.

84
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:21,873
For example, in a
hierarchical structure, there are

85
00:06:21,874 --> 00:06:24,991
horizontal and vertical
levels within the organisation.

86
00:06:25,310 --> 00:06:29,404
These hierarchical levels
range from the line management

87
00:06:29,405 --> 00:06:32,410
level through to the
executive management level.

88
00:06:32,411 --> 00:06:37,270
The responsibility, accountability and
authority assigned to the hierarchical

89
00:06:37,271 --> 00:06:40,912
level indicate how the
individual may perform the

90
00:06:40,913 --> 00:06:43,901
noted function within that
organisational structure.

91
00:06:44,510 --> 00:06:47,210
Number four, organisational structure
types.

92
00:06:47,830 --> 00:06:50,548
Please read the list of
factors in organisation structure

93
00:06:50,549 --> 00:06:56,930
selection and study table 2,
1, on PIMBOC guide page 47.

94
00:06:57,390 --> 00:06:59,808
It compares several
types of organizational

95
00:06:59,809 --> 00:07:02,061
structures and their
influence and projects.

96
00:07:02,410 --> 00:07:06,510
There is no one-size-fits-all structure
for any organisation.

97
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:09,602
The final structure for
a given organisation is

98
00:07:09,603 --> 00:07:12,570
unique due to the numerous
variables to be considered.

99
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:13,490
3.

100
00:07:13,990 --> 00:07:20,450
Project Management Office Pinball Guide
defines a project management office as an

101
00:07:20,451 --> 00:07:25,010
organisational structure that standardises
the project related governance processes

102
00:07:25,011 --> 00:07:30,730
and facilitates the sharing of resources,
methodologies, tools and techniques.

103
00:07:30,731 --> 00:07:37,570
The responsibilities of a PMO can range
from providing project management support

104
00:07:37,571 --> 00:07:41,550
functions to the direct management of one
or more projects.

105
00:07:42,530 --> 00:07:46,810
There are several types of PMO in
organisations.

106
00:07:47,610 --> 00:07:50,810
Each type varies in the degree of control
and influence.

107
00:07:51,330 --> 00:07:55,450
It has some projects within the
organisation, such as supportive.

108
00:07:55,610 --> 00:08:02,010
Supportive PMOs provide a consultative
role to projects, by supplying templates,

109
00:08:02,190 --> 00:08:05,019
best practices, training,
access to information

110
00:08:05,020 --> 00:08:07,844
and lessons learned from other projects.

111
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:12,630
This type of PMO serves as a project
repository.

112
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:17,410
The degree of control provided by this
type of PMO is low.

113
00:08:18,630 --> 00:08:21,050
And now we move on to controlling PMO.

114
00:08:21,610 --> 00:08:26,070
It provides support and requires
compliance through various means.

115
00:08:26,570 --> 00:08:29,570
The degree of control provided by the PMO
is moderate.

116
00:08:30,350 --> 00:08:36,510
Compliance involve adoption of project
management frameworks and methodologies,

117
00:08:36,830 --> 00:08:40,110
use of specific templates, forms and
tools.

118
00:08:40,590 --> 00:08:42,247
And by that they, I mean they actually

119
00:08:42,248 --> 00:08:45,344
mandate the use of
those templates and tools.

120
00:08:45,470 --> 00:08:48,090
Conformance to governance frameworks.

121
00:08:48,870 --> 00:08:51,210
Then we come to directive PMOs.

122
00:08:51,930 --> 00:08:55,870
They take control of projects by directly
managing the projects.

123
00:08:56,350 --> 00:08:59,660
Project managers are assigned by and
report to the PMO.

124
00:08:59,661 --> 00:09:04,140
The degree of control provided by the PMO
is high.

125
00:09:05,020 --> 00:09:09,540
The project management office may have
organizational wide responsibilities.

126
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:12,588
It may play a role in supporting strategic

127
00:09:12,589 --> 00:09:15,680
alignment and delivering
organizational value.

128
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:22,200
The PMO integrates data and information
from organizational strategic projects and

129
00:09:22,201 --> 00:09:26,660
evaluates how higher level strategic
projects are being fulfilled.

130
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:33,200
The PMO is the natural liaison between the
project's portfolios, programs,

131
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:36,900
projects and the organisational measuring
systems, e.g.

132
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:38,580
Balance Scorecard.

133
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:44,700
The PMO may manage programs of related
projects or portfolios of unrelated

134
00:09:44,701 --> 00:09:48,704
projects and work, but always
with the view to providing

135
00:09:48,705 --> 00:09:52,120
efficiencies and benefits
of a common administration.

136
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:59,360
A PMO may have the authority to act as an
integral stakeholder and is a key

137
00:09:59,361 --> 00:10:02,465
decision-maker throughout
the life of each project in

138
00:10:02,466 --> 00:10:05,301
order to keep it aligned
with the business objectives.

139
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:12,260
The PMO may make recommendations,
lead knowledge transfer, terminate

140
00:10:12,261 --> 00:10:15,060
projects and take other actions as
required.

141
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:21,480
A primary function of a PMO is to support
project managers in a variety of ways,

142
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:26,460
which may include managing shared
resources across all projects administered

143
00:10:26,461 --> 00:10:31,160
by the PMO, identifying and developing
project management methodology,

144
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:36,320
best practices and standards, coaching,
mentoring, training and oversight,

145
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:41,560
monitoring with project management
standards, policies, procedures and

146
00:10:41,561 --> 00:10:47,020
templates by means of project audits,
developing and managing project policies,

147
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:50,607
procedures, templates and
other shared documentation,

148
00:10:50,608 --> 00:10:53,552
organizational process assets
and coordinating communication and

149
00:10:53,553 --> 00:10:56,194
coordinating communication
across projects.

150
00:10:56,980 --> 00:11:01,520
That brings us to the end of
organizational systems and also to the end

151
00:11:01,521 --> 00:11:04,540
of the environments in which projects
operate.

152
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:10,200
And the next module is 3.0, the role of
the project manager.

153
